{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:23022501 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:23022501 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"23022501","source":"MED","pmid":"23022501","doi":"10.1016/j.neulet.2012.09.039","title":"Behavioral changes and brain energy metabolism dysfunction in rats treated with methamphetamine or dextroamphetamine.","authorString":"Feier G, Valvassori SS, Lopes-Borges J, Varela RB, Bavaresco DV, Scaini G, Morais MO, Andersen ML, Streck EL, Quevedo J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Feier G","firstName":"Gustavo","lastName":"Feier","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Neurosciences and National Institute for Translational Medicine (INCT-TM) and Center of Excellence in Applied Neurosciences of Santa Catarina (NENASC), Postgraduate Program in Health Sciences, Health Sciences Unit, University of Southern Santa Catarina, Criciúma, SC, Brazil."}]}},{"fullName":"Valvassori SS","firstName":"Samira S","lastName":"Valvassori","initials":"SS"},{"fullName":"Lopes-Borges J","firstName":"Jéssica","lastName":"Lopes-Borges","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Varela RB","firstName":"Roger B","lastName":"Varela","initials":"RB","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7995-5904"}},{"fullName":"Bavaresco DV","firstName":"Daniela V","lastName":"Bavaresco","initials":"DV"},{"fullName":"Scaini G","firstName":"Giselli","lastName":"Scaini","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9880-0887"}},{"fullName":"Morais MO","firstName":"Meline O","lastName":"Morais","initials":"MO"},{"fullName":"Andersen ML","firstName":"Monica L","lastName":"Andersen","initials":"ML"},{"fullName":"Streck EL","firstName":"Emilio L","lastName":"Streck","initials":"EL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2859-4678"}},{"fullName":"Quevedo J","firstName":"João","lastName":"Quevedo","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3114-6611"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1894-6748"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2859-4678"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5533-8848"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7995-5904"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9880-0887"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3114-6611"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4824-7742"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"530","journalIssueId":1980092,"dateOfPublication":"2012 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2012,"printPublicationDate":"2012-11-01","journal":{"title":"Neuroscience letters","medlineAbbreviation":"Neurosci Lett","isoabbreviation":"Neurosci Lett","nlmid":"7600130","essn":"1872-7972","issn":"0304-3940"}},"pubYear":"2012","pageInfo":"75-79","abstractText":"Studies have demonstrated that AMPHs produce long-term damage to the brain dopaminergic, serotoninergic and glutamatergic regions. Prefrontal cortex, amygdala, hippocampus and striatum appear to be involved in the toxicity and behavioral changes induced by AMPHs. A single dose of AMPH causes mitochondrial dysfunction and oxidative stress in rat brain. The goal of the present study was thus to investigate the potency of two amphetamines, dextroamphetamine (d-AMPH) and methamphetamine (m-AMPH), on the behavior and energetic dysfunction in the brain of rats. d-AMPH and m-AMPH increased the crossing and rearing behaviors. The numbers of visits to the center were increased by d-AMPH and m-AMPH only at 2mg/kg. Likewise, at a high dose (2 mg/kg), the injection of m-AMPH increased the amount of sniffing. The AMPHs significantly decreased the activities of Krebs cycle enzymes (citrate synthase and succinate dehydrogenase) and mitochondrial respiratory chain complexes (I-IV); nevertheless, this effect varied depending on the brain region evaluated. In summary, this study demonstrated that at high doses, m-AMPH, increased stereotyped (sniffing) behavior in rats, but d-AMPH did not. However, this study shows that d-AMPH and m-AMPH seem to have similar effects on the brains energetic metabolism.","affiliation":"Laboratory of Neurosciences and National Institute for Translational Medicine (INCT-TM) and Center of Excellence in Applied Neurosciences of Santa Catarina (NENASC), Postgraduate Program in Health Sciences, Health Sciences Unit, University of Southern Santa Catarina, Criciúma, SC, Brazil.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Brain","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects","majorTopic_YN":"Y"},{"abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Amygdala","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Hippocampus","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Corpus Striatum","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Prefrontal Cortex","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mitochondria","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"d